\chapter*{Abstract}
% the \makeabstract command creates the top portion of the abstract
% page ... must be issued before the abstract content
\makeabstract

%%%%%%%%%%% Your Abstract Text Goes after Here %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

The principle objective of this dissertation is to analyze the NASA
Magnetohydrodynamic Augmented Propulsion Experiment (MAPX) using a
three-dimensional numerical model.  The results of this analysis are
intended to increase the understanding of the of the critical
physical processes in the accelerator and to provide pre-test
performance predictions.  Because of the three-dimensional nature of
the electromagnetic forces involved, one must use a
three-dimensional numerical model to properly analyze any
magnetohydrodynamic (MHD) flow.  However, no such three-dimensional
model existed for diagonal MHD accelerators.

Therefore, we developed a parabolic three-dimensional numerical
model capable of simulating diagonal MHD accelerator flows.  The
magnetohydrodynamic equation set comprised the mass continuity
equation, three momentum equations, the energy equation, two
turbulence equations, and Maxwell's electrical equations.  This
numerical model incorporates the NASA Chemical Equilibrium with
Applications code for calculation of thermodynamic and species
concentration properties and uses a numerical technique based on
electron-neutral momentum transfer cross-sections to calculated
electrical conductivity.  This model is advantageous because it
allows for the simulation of partially ionized plasma flows.

This new three-dimensional diagonal MHD accelerator model was then
used to analyze the NASA MAPX accelerator.  From this 3-D analysis,
contrary to the previous one-dimensional analyses, we recommend that
the MAPX accelerator operate at a 45-degree angle, with an applied
current of 100-Amps, for a length of .800m.  The 45-degree angle
represents that maximum that can be achieved experimentally,
allowing for a reduction in Hall current.  The 100-Amp applied
current allows for  high accelerator exit velocity while preventing
electrode damage due to over-powering.  We also determined that, due
to secondary flows in the MHD accelerator, powering the electrodes
more than 0.800m results in a reversal of the Faraday current,
essentially transforming the accelerator into a generator, which
results in a reduction in velocity.

This research marks the first in-depth, three dimensional numerical
analysis of an experimental diagonal MHD accelerator.  The results
of this research have helped to define the experimental
configuration of the NASA MAPX accelerator and offer a better
understanding of the critical physical processes that occur within a
diagonal magnetohydrodynamic accelerator.

